<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich Hecker</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1811">1811</a>–<a href="/years/1881">1881</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich Hecker was a lawyer and radical democrat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> who led an armed rising for a German republic in April 1848, the Hecker Uprising, which was defeated near Kandern. He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where he farmed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> and served as a colonel in the Union army during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-hecker.jpg" alt="Friedrich Hecker" width="200" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fr_Hecker_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hecker was born at Eichtersheim, now Angelbachtal, the son of a revenue official, and studied law at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-university">University of Heidelberg</a>. In 1838 he was an advocate before the Supreme Court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a>. Elected to the Second Chamber of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in 1842, he joined the opposition at once and helped to oust the Blittersdorf ministry. A speech of 6 February 1845 against the incorporation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> made him known beyond Baden, and a democratic campaign with Johann Adam von Itzstein led to his expulsion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-struve">Gustav von Struve</a> he drew up the radical programme adopted at Offenburg on 12 September 1847. In the preliminary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt Parliament</a> he moved that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> be declared a republic, without success. After the arrest of a friend, the editor of the Constanzer Seeblatt, on 8 April 1848, he and Struve summoned armed men to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donaueschingen">Donaueschingen</a> for 14 April; they expected 70,000 but only a few thousand came. Troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> under General Friedrich von Gagern defeated them near Kandern on 20 April, though Gagern was killed.</p><p>From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> of Basel he published a radical newspaper and wrote Die Volkserhebung in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. Refused admission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt Parliament</a> though twice elected for Thiengen, he emigrated in September 1848 and bought a farm near Belleville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>. In 1856 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> were chosen as Illinois Republican electors-at-large. In the Civil War he commanded the 24th Illinois Infantry Regiment until December 1861 and from October 1862 the 82nd Illinois, and was badly wounded at Chancellorsville on 2 May 1863.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1864 Hecker led a faction of Frémont supporters against Lincoln's renomination. After the war he returned to his farm, wrote for the German-language press and in 1873 visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where he criticised the new state's lack of individual rights. He died at his farm in Summerfield, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, on 24 March 1881. The Heckerlied and the Hecker hat, a broad slouch hat with a feather, became symbols of middle-class political activism, and a monument to him was installed in Benton Park, St. Louis, on 1 October 1882.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Friedrich Hecker” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Germany <b>Friedrich Hecker</b> and Gustav Struve led a radical republican uprising in Baden in April 1848, Archduke John of Austria served as Imperial Regent, and Robert Blum was executed by Austrian troops.</blockquote><a href="/movement/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848 · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The Baden revolutionaries of 1848, led by <b>Friedrich Hecker</b> and Gustav Struve, were defeated, and in 1849 the army mutinied and the grand duke fled until Prussian troops restored him.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-baden">Grand Duchy of Baden</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Eichtersheim, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Summerfield, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.23,8.78&amp;year=1856" aria-label="Eichtersheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4752 447 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Friedrich Hecker"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5202 717h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5202 717h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.23,8.78&amp;year=1856">What was here in 1856? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
